WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.790
On 4 November 1945, Hungarians chose freely among several parties, and the Communists won 17 percent.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:17.660
On 15 May 1949, voters got a single list, and a ballot with no mark on it counted as a yes.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.530
Officially, 95.6 percent of those who voted backed the list.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:32.210
In between, Hungary lost its freedom slice by slice. This is how, and why it still mattered in October 1956.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:46.020
Hungary had gone to war on Germany's side, and in March 1944 German troops occupied the country.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:54.360
That year, about 437,000 Jews were deported from Hungary, most of them to be murdered at Auschwitz.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:02.530
In the autumn, the Red Army fought its way in, and Budapest was besieged from December until February 1945.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:09.520
By April, the Germans had been driven out, and an estimated 40 percent of the nation's wealth was destroyed.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.360
The Soviet soldiers who drove them out also looted, and very many women were raped.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:23.650
Some 600,000 Hungarians, soldiers and civilians, were taken to Soviet camps, and about a third never came home.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:30.970
The historian György Gyarmati calls it a half liberation, free of the Nazis but occupied.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:37.660
In January 1945, a provisional government formed in Debrecen signed an armistice.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:44.510
Real power now lay with the Allied Control Commission, chaired by the Soviet marshal Kliment Voroshilov.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:54.710
In March 1945, a land reform broke up the great estates, and some 640,000 poor peasants received land.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:05.400
On 4 November 1945, six parties approved by the Control Commission stood in a general election.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:11.090
The ballot was secret, and 92.5 percent of registered voters took part.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:17.080
The Independent Smallholders' Party won about 57 percent, a majority on its own.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:24.750
The Social Democrats won 17.4 percent, and the Communists of Mátyás Rákosi 17.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:32.420
The American representative in Budapest reported no evidence that the election was anything but free.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:38.610
But the Allies required a coalition, and only 9 of its 18 ministers were Smallholders.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:43.880
Voroshilov vetoed a Smallholder at the Interior Ministry, which ran the police.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:49.950
It went to the Communist Imre Nagy, the man who would lead the government in 1956.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:56.490
The political police, run by Gábor Péter, answered to the Communists from the start.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:04.210
In February 1946, Hungary became a republic, with the Smallholder Zoltán Tildy as president.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:13.660
Meanwhile, money collapsed. By July 1946, prices doubled about every 15 hours, the worst inflation ever recorded.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.460
In August, the forint replaced the worthless pengő.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:27.750
Rákosi later called his method salami tactics: cutting rivals away one slice at a time.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:36.370
In late 1946, the political police announced a conspiracy against the republic, and arrested Smallholder deputies.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:43.270
Their confessions were forced out of them by torture, at the political police headquarters on Andrássy Avenue.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:54.660
Parliament refused to lift the immunity of the party's secretary general, Béla Kovács, so Soviet soldiers arrested him on 25 February 1947.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.030
He spent eight years in Soviet prisons and camps.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:05.880
In May, Prime Minister Ferenc Nagy, accused while on holiday in Switzerland, resigned and stayed abroad.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:15.770
Before the next election, on 31 August 1947, about 467,000 people were struck off the voters' rolls.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:24.460
On the day, Communist activists drove from station to station, voting again and again with blue slips meant for voters away from home.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:33.930
The number of false votes is disputed. The police chief counted nearly 63,000, and other estimates go up to about 200,000.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:39.330
The Communists came first with 22 percent, and the Smallholders fell to 15.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:48.410
Opposition parties still won about 40 percent between them. Then the Independence Party's 49 seats were annulled.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:05:01.920
The Communists called 1948 the year of the turn. On 25 March, every factory with more than 100 workers was taken over in a single day.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:10.640
In June, the Social Democrats were merged into the Communist Party, which became the Hungarian Working People's Party, the MDP.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:18.460
Social Democrats who refused, like Anna Kéthly, were pushed out, and President Tildy was forced to resign.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:26.030
On 15 May 1949, voters got one list, the People's Front's, drawn up by the MDP.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:30.480
A yes needed no mark: the ballot went into the box as it was.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.800
A no meant crossing out a circle with two lines, behind a curtain, where taking too long could be noted.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:45.400
On 20 August 1949, a constitution on the Soviet model made Hungary a People's Republic.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:52.150
A new state emblem with a red star replaced the old Kossuth arms, on flags too.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:05.770
On 26 December 1948, police arrested Cardinal József Mindszenty, head of Hungary's Catholics, and he was sentenced to life.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:14.910
In 1948, Stalin had broken with Tito, Yugoslavia's Communist leader, and every Soviet ally now hunted for Titoists.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:22.630
Hungary's chosen victim was László Rajk, a veteran of the Spanish Civil War and a former interior minister.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:31.080
He was charged with spying for the West and plotting with Tito, and his trial in September 1949 went out on the radio.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.770
Rajk confessed to everything. Every question and every answer had been written in advance.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:46.720
Witnesses later told the UN that the accused had been tortured, and that János Kádár, the interior minister, had promised Rajk his life for a confession.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:56.210
On 15 October 1949, Rajk was hanged with Tibor Szőnyi and András Szalai. Secret side trials followed.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:02.760
Rákosi told the party to look behind every mistake for the hand of the enemy.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:12.630
The lesson was plain: nobody was safe. In 1951, Kádár himself was arrested, and later sentenced to life on invented charges.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:20.780
Only in March 1956 did the regime admit that the charges against Rajk had been fabricated.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:30.770
By the early 1950s, power lay with one party, and within it with one man, Mátyás Rákosi.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:36.920
His portrait hung beside Stalin's, and the press called him Stalin's best Hungarian disciple.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:44.960
For his 60th birthday, on 9 March 1952, gifts from all over the country filled a palace in Budapest.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.850
That August, he became prime minister as well.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:55.370
The five-year plan drove heavy industry, and a new steelworks on the Danube was named after Stalin.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:08:05.820
Peasants were driven into collective farms, the ÁVH watched everyone, and by 1953 real wages were a fifth lower than in 1949.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:15.540
On 5 March 1953, Stalin died. Three days later, Hungary's parliament wrote his immortal memory into law.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:22.910
In eight years, a free vote had become a single list, and a coalition a dictatorship, guarded by Soviet troops.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:30.930
Terror, deportations, poverty and foreign troops would bring Hungarians onto the streets in October 1956.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:37.650
And they would cut the red-star emblem out of their flags. Why they rose is the next story.
